"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,610\nThe Learning Brain. Series 8: Assessment That Teaches\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,331\nEpisode 10\n\n3\n00:00:07,411 --> 00:00:09,832\nMake the task transparent\n\n4\n00:00:12,632 --> 00:00:17,326\nAn assignment says: write a ten page paper on a topic of your choice.\n\n5\n00:00:17,613 --> 00:00:20,270\nSome students know exactly what that means.\n\n6\n00:00:20,606 --> 00:00:23,252\nOthers have no idea what a good one looks like.\n\n7\n00:00:23,422 --> 00:00:26,327\nThe Transparency in Learning and Teaching project,\n\n8\n00:00:26,703 --> 00:00:33,720\nstarted by Mary Ann Winkelmes at the University of Chicago and expanded at the University of Illinois,\n\n9\n00:00:34,092 --> 00:00:38,063\nasks instructors to make three things explicit for each assignment: its purpose,\n\n10\n00:00:38,558 --> 00:00:42,681\nthe task, and the criteria for success.\n\n11\n00:00:42,851 --> 00:00:46,016\nIn a national study published in 2016,\n\n12\n00:00:46,484 --> 00:00:53,031\ninstructors at minority serving institutions made just two assignments more transparent during a term.\n\n13\n00:00:53,201 --> 00:00:58,260\nStudents in the more transparent courses reported greater academic confidence,\n\n14\n00:00:58,549 --> 00:01:00,441\na stronger sense of belonging,\n\n15\n00:01:00,663 --> 00:01:03,275\nand more awareness of the skills they were building.\n\n16\n00:01:03,895 --> 00:01:06,730\nThe benefits were larger for first generation,\n\n17\n00:01:07,029 --> 00:01:10,037\nlow income and underrepresented students.\n\n18\n00:01:10,207 --> 00:01:12,274\nWhy would clarity matter so much?\n\n19\n00:01:12,598 --> 00:01:17,126\nSome students arrive knowing the unwritten rules of college assignments.\n\n20\n00:01:17,519 --> 00:01:21,921\nOthers spend their energy decoding the instructions instead of doing the work.\n\n21\n00:01:22,091 --> 00:01:23,068\nSome caution.\n\n22\n00:01:23,482 --> 00:01:25,894\nMany of the outcomes were self reported,\n\n23\n00:01:26,059 --> 00:01:28,716\nand the studies were not randomized trials.\n\n24\n00:01:29,107 --> 00:01:30,810\nBut the change is small,\n\n25\n00:01:30,830 --> 00:01:35,348\nlow cost, and consistent with the belonging research in Series five.\n\n26\n00:01:35,518 --> 00:01:38,409\nRubrics and examples are part of transparency.\n\n27\n00:01:38,811 --> 00:01:40,689\nSharing what good work looks like,\n\n28\n00:01:40,843 --> 00:01:43,123\nand why, helps students aim for it.\n\n29\n00:01:43,293 --> 00:01:44,077\nFor teaching.\n\n30\n00:01:44,453 --> 00:01:45,772\nFor your next assignment,\n\n31\n00:01:45,792 --> 00:01:47,776\nwrite one sentence on its purpose,\n\n32\n00:01:47,957 --> 00:01:49,319\na short list of steps,\n\n33\n00:01:49,339 --> 00:01:51,350\nand the criteria you will use.\n\n34\n00:01:51,669 --> 00:01:53,631\nShare an annotated example.\n\n35\n00:01:53,900 --> 00:01:56,441\nDiscuss it in class before students begin.\n\n36\n00:01:56,842 --> 00:01:58,741\nOne tip to share with students: ask why this task matters,\n\n37\n00:01:58,963 --> 00:02:01,853\nand what good looks like.\n\n38\n00:02:02,023 --> 00:02:02,882\nSay why.\n\n39\n00:02:02,886 --> 00:02:03,595\nSay what.\n\n40\n00:02:03,762 --> 00:02:05,313\nSay how it will be judged.\n\n41\n00:02:05,554 --> 00:02:07,895\nThen let students show what they can do.\n\n"